(5746876, 835051, 80904, '<td valign="top">What no Landscape of the Houston Area???<br/> <br/>Congrats on the HM. Great Image.</td>', 'What no Landscape of the Houston Area??? Congrats on the HM. Great Image.', '2009-11-30 13:57:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746907, 835107, 65319, '<td valign="top">Mark, what I find quite outstanding for this photo is that the dark curve pattern in the grass matches the curve pattern of light in the sky which creates a wonderful sense of rhythm in this photo...along with the foreground interest (cool grass).  The colors are strong and beautifully purple!  However, my only \"nattering naybob nitpick\" would be the tones in the cloud sky seem a bit heavy.  Beautiful and lovely photo, Mark!</td>', 'Mark, what I find quite outstanding for this photo is that the dark curve pattern in the grass matches the curve pattern of light in the sky which creates a wonderful sense of rhythm in this photo...along with the foreground interest (cool grass).  The colors are strong and beautifully purple!  However, my only \"nattering naybob nitpick\" would be the tones in the cloud sky seem a bit heavy.  Beautiful and lovely photo, Mark!', '2009-11-30 14:26:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746911, 834907, 100393, '<td valign="top">Responding to your request for comments. I gave you a 4; I''ll try and explain why:<br/> <br/>Firstly, I certainly didn''t see the ''eating'' I do now but for me interesting landscape doesn''t necessarily make for an interesting image and given the mountains are your primary focal interest point, I think they aren''t really interesting enough to make up for a lack of photographic interest.<br/> <br/>I do like the sky and the water - especially the gradated colour but the pureness of the water is ruined by the messy foreground and it may just be the light but the saturation of the foreground bush seems uncomfortably high.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, I think landscapes need to communicate some depth and leading lines going ''into'' the image really help - I don''t see any here.<br/> <br/>Trying to including a foreground element with a background at this distance was always going to give you some focus difficulties - I haven''t really got my head around the whole hyperfocal thing but I''m pretty sure you''ve made things difficult for yourself here.<br/> <br/>I hope this helps<br/> <br/>Paul</td>', 'Responding to your request for comments. I gave you a 4; I''ll try and explain why: Firstly, I certainly didn''t see the ''eating'' I do now but for me interesting landscape doesn''t necessarily make for an interesting image and given the mountains are your primary focal interest point, I think they aren''t really interesting enough to make up for a lack of photographic interest. I do like the sky and the water - especially the gradated colour but the pureness of the water is ruined by the messy foreground and it may just be the light but the saturation of the foreground bush seems uncomfortably high. Compositionally, I think landscapes need to communicate some depth and leading lines going ''into'' the image really help - I don''t see any here. Trying to including a foreground element with a background at this distance was always going to give you some focus difficulties - I haven''t really got my head around the whole hyperfocal thing but I''m pretty sure you''ve made things difficult for yourself here. I hope this helps Paul', '2009-11-30 14:33:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746938, 834683, 39427, '<td valign="top">WELL DONE!</td>', 'WELL DONE!', '2009-11-30 14:56:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746940, 834612, 39427, '<td valign="top">Obsessed with the tones you''ve presented, I absolutely need to learn to do this, huge congrats</td>', 'Obsessed with the tones you''ve presented, I absolutely need to learn to do this, huge congrats', '2009-11-30 14:57:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746943, 834684, 39427, '<td valign="top">so good... instant fave</td>', 'so good... instant fave', '2009-11-30 14:58:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746946, 834907, 7778, '<td valign="top">A quiet, static scape with good complimenting colours. As it is, an empty blue sky consumes nearly half of the image; gradating it along with the foreground water, as you did, has improved the givens considerably. <br/> <br/>Still, the horizon lies too close to the horizontal centre for a dynamic composition, especially since the (opposite) shore drops it even lower. As others have observed, the frontmost shrub has more attention than an incidental obstruction should have, attention the small tufts of sedge in the water critically need. <br/> <br/>I think the inclusion of an interesting foreground object is critical for such an expansive, cloudless setting. There is no bird either, no moon to extend the view beyond the horizon and back, in the form of a reflection, onto the water. <br/> <br/>For these reasons, I much prefer your outtake, the higher horizon, even the subdued colours and contrast , which render the light less harsh and leave us a moodier maritime scene. <br/>The symbolism of mountain-fish swallowing another is, IMO, delicately reserved for those who find enough pleasure here to linger for a while.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-12-01 11:40:31.</i></td>', 'A quiet, static scape with good complimenting colours. As it is, an empty blue sky consumes nearly half of the image; gradating it along with the foreground water, as you did, has improved the givens considerably.  Still, the horizon lies too close to the horizontal centre for a dynamic composition, especially since the (opposite) shore drops it even lower. As others have observed, the frontmost shrub has more attention than an incidental obstruction should have, attention the small tufts of sedge in the water critically need.  I think the inclusion of an interesting foreground object is critical for such an expansive, cloudless setting. There is no bird either, no moon to extend the view beyond the horizon and back, in the form of a reflection, onto the water.  For these reasons, I much prefer your outtake, the higher horizon, even the subdued colours and contrast , which render the light less harsh and leave us a moodier maritime scene. The symbolism of mountain-fish swallowing another is, IMO, delicately reserved for those who find enough pleasure here to linger for a while.', '2009-11-30 14:59:01.000000', '2009-12-01 11:40:31.000000', 0),
(5746956, 835098, 39427, '<td valign="top">duds this is so cool and abstract, i can''t even tell what i''m looking at but i love the natural forms.  You must tell me where you went!</td>', 'duds this is so cool and abstract, i can''t even tell what i''m looking at but i love the natural forms.  You must tell me where you went!', '2009-11-30 15:02:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(5746995, 835107, 42013, '<td valign="top">Thanks Mae and everyone else for your comments. I intended the colors to be over the top and I was going for a heavy contrast but probably went a little overboard! I tried an alternate warm toned BW conversion but couldn''t get anything that looked good.<br/> <br/>Oh, and I did emphasize the dark patterns in the field with a little local dodge/burn :)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-11-30 15:20:40.</i></td>', 'Thanks Mae and everyone else for your comments. I intended the colors to be over the top and I was going for a heavy contrast but probably went a little overboard! I tried an alternate warm toned BW conversion but couldn''t get anything that looked good. Oh, and I did emphasize the dark patterns in the field with a little local dodge/burn :)', '2009-11-30 15:20:06.000000', '2009-11-30 15:20:40.000000', 0),
